What is GitHub? 
GitHub is a cloud-based platform that hosts Git repositories.It's a digital workspace where developers can store their code, track every change they make over time, and collaborate with others without overwriting each other's work. 

The GitHub Workflow (GitHub 工作流)
1. Create a Branch
Create a separate branch from the main project.
2. Make Changes & Commit
Edit your files, then save a "snapshot" of your changes with a descriptive message explaining why you made them.
3. Push to GitHub
Upload your branch and commits from your computer to the cloud (GitHub).
4. Open a Pull Request
Formally request to merge your branch into the main project. This starts a review process where teammates can comment and suggest changes.
5. Review & Discuss
Team members review your code, ask questions, and give feedback. You can update your branch with new commits based on their suggestions.
6. Merge
Once approved, click the merge button to officially integrate your changes into the main branch.
7. Delete the Branch
Clean up by deleting the branch you just merged. This keeps the repository tidy for future work.

The core GitHub workflow is a cycle: Issue → Branch → Commit → Pull Request → Merge → Delete.
